{{also|parlor|pärlor|parlør}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[parlour]] {{qualifier|British}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|parlur|lang=fro}} and Old French {{term|parleor|lang=fro}}, from the verb {{term|parler||to speak|lang=fro}}. <!-- or {{etyl|ang|en}} ''[[parlour]], [[parlur]]'', {{etyl|fr|en}} ''[[parloir]]'', {{etyl|LL.|en}} ''[[parlatorium]]''. ? -->

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈpɑːlə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈpɑɹləɹ/}}
* {{audio|En-us-parlour.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɑː(r)lə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[living room]] of a [[house]], or a room for entertaining guests; a room for talking.
#*{{quote-book|year=1913|author={{w|Joseph C. Lincoln}}|chapter=12
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5535161W Mr. Pratt's Patients]
|passage=So, after a spell, he decided to make the best of it and shoved us into the front '''parlor'''. 'Twas a dismal sort of place, with hair wreaths, and wax fruit, and tin lambrekins, and land knows what all.}}
# {{label|en|archaic}} The apartment in a monastery or nunnery where the residents are permitted to meet and converse with each other or with visitors from the outside.
# A room for lounging; a [[sitting-room]]; a [[drawing room]].
# {{label|en|archaic}} A [[comfortable]] [[room]] in a [[public house]].
# {{label|en|chiefly|Southern US}} A covered open-air [[patio]].
# A [[shop]] or other [[business]] selling goods specified by context.
# A [[shed]] used for [[milking]] [[cattle]].

